{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CxR6UDZo1kFkiUY7sA4MDD","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Current Evaluation State\n     2→\n     3→## Active Text\n     4→- **Name:** masque_of_red_death\n     5→- **Attempt:** 13\n     6→- **Phase:** awaiting_fix\n     7→- **baseline_score:** 6.75\n     8→\n     9→## Latest Scores\n    10→- Structure Detection: 10/10\n    11→- Character Extraction: 3/10 ← CRITICAL FAILURE\n    12→- Character Profiles: 6/10\n    13→- Chapter Summaries: 9/10\n    14→- Pronunciation Guide: 6/10\n    15→- HTML Presentation: 8/10\n    16→- **Overall: 6.85/10** (threshold: 8.0)\n    17→\n    18→## Score Delta from Baseline (Attempt 1)\n    19→- Structure: 10 -> 10 (unchanged)\n    20→- Characters: 5 -> 3 (**-2 REGRESSION** from attempt 1)\n    21→- Profiles: 2 -> 6 (+4 improvement)\n    22→- Summaries: 9 -> 9 (unchanged)\n    23→- Pronunciation: 5 -> 6 (+1 improvement)\n    24→- Presentation: 9 -> 8 (-1 regression)\n    25→- **Overall: 6.75 -> 6.85 (+0.10 slight improvement)**\n    26→\n    27→## Attempt 13 Analysis: ENHANCED DEATH DETECTION FAILED\n    28→\n    29→### What Was Tried\n    30→Enhanced `_entities_in_death_relationship()` in `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` to use multiple name variants and improved matching patterns.\n    31→\n    32→### Result\n    33→**FAILED** - The merge STILL occurs with the production model (qwen3-next:80b).\n    34→\n    35→### Key Finding from Debug Logs\n    36→`_entities_in_death_relationship()` is **NOT being called at all**.\n    37→\n    38→This proves the merge is happening at a DIFFERENT level than cross-group resolution - likely at:\n    39→1. Pairwise alias resolution (`_llm_alias_resolution()` or `_heuristic_alias_resolution()`)\n    40→2. Or at the proposer level where \"the mummer\" is already associated with Prospero\n    41→\n    42→### The Smoking Gun (Unchanged from Previous Attempts)\n    43→**The chapter summary pipeline CORRECTLY identifies 2 characters:**\n    44→- \"Prince Prospero\"\n    45→- \"The masked figure (Red Death)\"\n    46→\n    47→**But the character extraction pipeline merges them into 1.**\n    48→\n    49→This proves the underlying LLM CAN distinguish these characters - the merge is happening in the character extraction consensus logic.\n    50→\n    51→## Current Issues (Priority Order)\n    52→\n    53→### CRITICAL\n    54→1. **False character merge: \"the mummer\" merged with Prince Prospero**\n    55→   - Problem: \"the mummer\" is listed as an alias of Prince Prospero, but it refers to the Red Death personification - THE MAIN ANTAGONIST\n    56→   - Evidence from text:\n    57→     - \"seizing the mummer, whose tall figure stood erect and motionless within the shadow of the ebony clock\"\n    58→     - \"fell prostrate in death the Prince Prospero\" (Prospero DIES after confronting the mummer)\n    59→     - The mummer is \"dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in grave-cloths\"\n    60→     - When unmasked, the figure is \"untenanted by any tangible form\" (not a person at all)\n    61→   - Impact: The main antagonist is merged with the protagonist (-2 point character score minimum)\n    62→   - **THIRTEEN attempts have now failed to fix this issue**\n    63→   - **Status:** Cross-group resolution fixes don't work because the merge happens BEFORE that stage\n    64→\n    65→2. **Missing character: The Red Death / Masked Figure**\n    66→   - Problem: The antagonist of the story should be its own character entry\n    67→   - Aliases that should be grouped together:\n    68→     - \"the figure\" / \"the masked figure\"\n    69→     - \"the stranger\"\n    70→     - \"the intruder\"\n    71→     - \"the mummer\"\n    72→     - \"the Red Death\" (personification)\n    73→   - Evidence: Chapter summary CORRECTLY identifies two characters but character extraction merges them\n    74→   - Impact: Major character missing from analysis\n    75→\n    76→### HIGH\n    77→3. **Missing alias: \"the duke\" for Prince Prospero**\n    78→   - Problem: Text uses \"the duke\" to refer to Prospero: \"as might have been expected from the duke's love of the bizarre\"\n    79→   - Location: Alias detection\n    80→\n    81→### MEDIUM\n    82→4. **Canonical name format includes leading article**\n    83→   - Problem: Character name is \"the Prince Prospero\" instead of \"Prince Prospero\"\n    84→   - Location: Character name normalization\n    85→\n    86→5. **Too many common words in pronunciation guide (65 in \"Other\")**\n    87→   - Problem: Common words like \"dauntless\", \"chiming\", \"magnificence\", \"casements\" are flagged\n    88→   - Location: Pronunciation flagging threshold\n    89→\n    90→6. **Foreign word false positive: \"decorum\"**\n    91→   - Problem: \"decorum\" flagged as foreign word - it's standard English (Latin-derived but fully assimilated)\n    92→   - Location: Foreign word detection\n    93→\n    94→## Root Cause Analysis: Summary After 13 Attempts\n    95→\n    96→### The Core Problem\n    97→The merge happens BEFORE cross-group resolution. Debug logging proves `_entities_in_death_relationship()` is never called, meaning \"the mummer\" and \"Prince Prospero\" are already in the same character group by the time cross-group resolution runs.\n    98→\n    99→### Where the Merge Is Happening (Investigation Needed)\n   100→Possible locations:\n   101→1. **`_is_descriptive_handle()` classification** - \"the mummer\" may be classified as a proper name rather than an epithet\n   102→2. **`_llm_alias_resolution()` or `_heuristic_alias_resolution()`** - The LLM may be deciding \"the mummer\" = \"Prince Prospero\" at the pairwise merge level\n   103→3. **Initial extraction** - Proposers may already be tagging \"the mummer\" with Prospero\n   104→\n   105→### What Has Been Tried (All Failed)\n   106→| Attempt | Fix Applied | Result |\n   107→|---------|-------------|--------|\n   108→| 1 | Cross-group epithet resolution | No effect |\n   109→| 2 | Proper name with article classification | Caused regression |\n   110→| 3 | CRITICAL RULE #5 about confrontation in prompt | No effect |\n   111→| 4 | `_entities_in_confrontation()` function | No effect |\n   112→| 5 | Solo pattern matching for confrontation | No effect |\n   113→| 6 | (Evaluation only) | No effect |\n   114→| 7 | Context window 150/120 -> 400 chars | No effect |\n   115→| 8 | Hard-coded death relationship detection | No effect |\n   116→| 9 | Context windows 600-800, death rule #6 | No effect |\n   117→| 10 | Death detection check ALL contexts | No effect |\n   118→| 11 | mention_context_chars 100 -> 200 | +0.15 score, merge still occurs |\n   119→| 12 | mention_context_chars 200 -> 250 | Works on small model, FAILS on production |\n   120→| 13 | Enhanced death relationship detection | Never called - merge happens earlier |\n   121→\n   122→## Fix History\n   123→\n   124→### Attempts 1-12\n   125→See previous EVALUATION_STATE.md entries and git history.\n   126→\n   127→### Attempt 13\n   128→- **Change:** Enhanced `_entities_in_death_relationship()` with multiple name variants\n   129→- **Result:** Function is NEVER CALLED - merge happens before cross-group resolution\n   130→- **Conclusion:** Must investigate WHERE the merge actually happens (pairwise level, not cross-group)\n   131→\n   132→## Recommended Next Approach (Attempt 14)\n   133→\n   134→### Priority 1: Add Debug Logging to Find Merge Location\n   135→Add logging to these functions to trace where \"the mummer\" gets merged with \"Prince Prospero\":\n   136→\n   137→1. **`_is_descriptive_handle()`** - Is \"the mummer\" classified as epithet or proper name?\n   138→2. **`_heuristic_alias_resolution()`** - Does heuristic logic merge them?\n   139→3. **`_llm_alias_resolution()`** - Does the LLM merge them at pairwise level?\n   140→4. **Initial extraction results** - Are they separate groups initially?\n   141→\n   142→### Priority 2: Post-Processing Character Split\n   143→Since the summary pipeline correctly identifies both characters, add a post-processing step:\n   144→1. Extract character names from chapter summaries (already available in `characters_present`)\n   145→2. Compare against final character list\n   146→3. If summary identifies more characters than extraction, investigate potential incorrect merges\n   147→4. Use death scene detection to SPLIT characters that were incorrectly merged\n   148→\n   149→### Priority 3: Classification Fix\n   150→If \"the mummer\" is being classified as a proper name:\n   151→- Make `_is_descriptive_handle()` more conservative about \"the X\" patterns\n   152→- \"the mummer\" should be classified as an epithet, not a proper name\n   153→- Epithets go through cross-group resolution (where death detection could work)\n   154→\n   155→## Output Files\n   156→- HTML: output/masque_of_red_death/report.html\n   157→- JSON: output/masque_of_red_death/analysis.json\n   158→\n   159→## Next Action\n   160→Run PROMPT_fix.md to:\n   161→1. First ADD DEBUG LOGGING to trace where the merge happens\n   162→2. Then apply targeted fix based on findings\n   163→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3924aa2c-d6fe-4686-86bb-94b756a35690","uuid":"9a0302d4-b6be-4644-8b3c-215bfdf7973b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/EVALUATION_STATE.md","content":"# Current Evaluation State\n\n## Active Text\n- **Name:** masque_of_red_death\n- **Attempt:** 13\n- **Phase:** awaiting_fix\n- **baseline_score:** 6.75\n\n## Latest Scores\n- Structure Detection: 10/10\n- Character Extraction: 3/10 ← CRITICAL FAILURE\n- Character Profiles: 6/10\n- Chapter Summaries: 9/10\n- Pronunciation Guide: 6/10\n- HTML Presentation: 8/10\n- **Overall: 6.85/10** (threshold: 8.0)\n\n## Score Delta from Baseline (Attempt 1)\n- Structure: 10 -> 10 (unchanged)\n- Characters: 5 -> 3 (**-2 REGRESSION** from attempt 1)\n- Profiles: 2 -> 6 (+4 improvement)\n- Summaries: 9 -> 9 (unchanged)\n- Pronunciation: 5 -> 6 (+1 improvement)\n- Presentation: 9 -> 8 (-1 regression)\n- **Overall: 6.75 -> 6.85 (+0.10 slight improvement)**\n\n## Attempt 13 Analysis: ENHANCED DEATH DETECTION FAILED\n\n### What Was Tried\nEnhanced `_entities_in_death_relationship()` in `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` to use multiple name variants and improved matching patterns.\n\n### Result\n**FAILED** - The merge STILL occurs with the production model (qwen3-next:80b).\n\n### Key Finding from Debug Logs\n`_entities_in_death_relationship()` is **NOT being called at all**.\n\nThis proves the merge is happening at a DIFFERENT level than cross-group resolution - likely at:\n1. Pairwise alias resolution (`_llm_alias_resolution()` or `_heuristic_alias_resolution()`)\n2. Or at the proposer level where \"the mummer\" is already associated with Prospero\n\n### The Smoking Gun (Unchanged from Previous Attempts)\n**The chapter summary pipeline CORRECTLY identifies 2 characters:**\n- \"Prince Prospero\"\n- \"The masked figure (Red Death)\"\n\n**But the character extraction pipeline merges them into 1.**\n\nThis proves the underlying LLM CAN distinguish these characters - the merge is happening in the character extraction consensus logic.\n\n## Current Issues (Priority Order)\n\n### CRITICAL\n1. **False character merge: \"the mummer\" merged with Prince Prospero**\n   - Problem: \"the mummer\" is listed as an alias of Prince Prospero, but it refers to the Red Death personification - THE MAIN ANTAGONIST\n   - Evidence from text:\n     - \"seizing the mummer, whose tall figure stood erect and motionless within the shadow of the ebony clock\"\n     - \"fell prostrate in death the Prince Prospero\" (Prospero DIES after confronting the mummer)\n     - The mummer is \"dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in grave-cloths\"\n     - When unmasked, the figure is \"untenanted by any tangible form\" (not a person at all)\n   - Impact: The main antagonist is merged with the protagonist (-2 point character score minimum)\n   - **THIRTEEN attempts have now failed to fix this issue**\n   - **Status:** Cross-group resolution fixes don't work because the merge happens BEFORE that stage\n\n2. **Missing character: The Red Death / Masked Figure**\n   - Problem: The antagonist of the story should be its own character entry\n   - Aliases that should be grouped together:\n     - \"the figure\" / \"the masked figure\"\n     - \"the stranger\"\n     - \"the intruder\"\n     - \"the mummer\"\n     - \"the Red Death\" (personification)\n   - Evidence: Chapter summary CORRECTLY identifies two characters but character extraction merges them\n   - Impact: Major character missing from analysis\n\n### HIGH\n3. **Missing alias: \"the duke\" for Prince Prospero**\n   - Problem: Text uses \"the duke\" to refer to Prospero: \"as might have been expected from the duke's love of the bizarre\"\n   - Location: Alias detection\n\n### MEDIUM\n4. **Canonical name format includes leading article**\n   - Problem: Character name is \"the Prince Prospero\" instead of \"Prince Prospero\"\n   - Location: Character name normalization\n\n5. **Too many common words in pronunciation guide (65 in \"Other\")**\n   - Problem: Common words like \"dauntless\", \"chiming\", \"magnificence\", \"casements\" are flagged\n   - Location: Pronunciation flagging threshold\n\n6. **Foreign word false positive: \"decorum\"**\n   - Problem: \"decorum\" flagged as foreign word - it's standard English (Latin-derived but fully assimilated)\n   - Location: Foreign word detection\n\n## Root Cause Analysis: Summary After 13 Attempts\n\n### The Core Problem\nThe merge happens BEFORE cross-group resolution. Debug logging proves `_entities_in_death_relationship()` is never called, meaning \"the mummer\" and \"Prince Prospero\" are already in the same character group by the time cross-group resolution runs.\n\n### Where the Merge Is Happening (Investigation Needed)\nPossible locations:\n1. **`_is_descriptive_handle()` classification** - \"the mummer\" may be classified as a proper name rather than an epithet\n2. **`_llm_alias_resolution()` or `_heuristic_alias_resolution()`** - The LLM may be deciding \"the mummer\" = \"Prince Prospero\" at the pairwise merge level\n3. **Initial extraction** - Proposers may already be tagging \"the mummer\" with Prospero\n\n### What Has Been Tried (All Failed)\n| Attempt | Fix Applied | Result |\n|---------|-------------|--------|\n| 1 | Cross-group epithet resolution | No effect |\n| 2 | Proper name with article classification | Caused regression |\n| 3 | CRITICAL RULE #5 about confrontation in prompt | No effect |\n| 4 | `_entities_in_confrontation()` function | No effect |\n| 5 | Solo pattern matching for confrontation | No effect |\n| 6 | (Evaluation only) | No effect |\n| 7 | Context window 150/120 -> 400 chars | No effect |\n| 8 | Hard-coded death relationship detection | No effect |\n| 9 | Context windows 600-800, death rule #6 | No effect |\n| 10 | Death detection check ALL contexts | No effect |\n| 11 | mention_context_chars 100 -> 200 | +0.15 score, merge still occurs |\n| 12 | mention_context_chars 200 -> 250 | Works on small model, FAILS on production |\n| 13 | Enhanced death relationship detection | Never called - merge happens earlier |\n\n## Fix History\n\n### Attempts 1-12\nSee previous EVALUATION_STATE.md entries and git history.\n\n### Attempt 13\n- **Change:** Enhanced `_entities_in_death_relationship()` with multiple name variants\n- **Result:** Function is NEVER CALLED - merge happens before cross-group resolution\n- **Conclusion:** Must investigate WHERE the merge actually happens (pairwise level, not cross-group)\n\n## Recommended Next Approach (Attempt 14)\n\n### Priority 1: Add Debug Logging to Find Merge Location\nAdd logging to these functions to trace where \"the mummer\" gets merged with \"Prince Prospero\":\n\n1. **`_is_descriptive_handle()`** - Is \"the mummer\" classified as epithet or proper name?\n2. **`_heuristic_alias_resolution()`** - Does heuristic logic merge them?\n3. **`_llm_alias_resolution()`** - Does the LLM merge them at pairwise level?\n4. **Initial extraction results** - Are they separate groups initially?\n\n### Priority 2: Post-Processing Character Split\nSince the summary pipeline correctly identifies both characters, add a post-processing step:\n1. Extract character names from chapter summaries (already available in `characters_present`)\n2. Compare against final character list\n3. If summary identifies more characters than extraction, investigate potential incorrect merges\n4. Use death scene detection to SPLIT characters that were incorrectly merged\n\n### Priority 3: Classification Fix\nIf \"the mummer\" is being classified as a proper name:\n- Make `_is_descriptive_handle()` more conservative about \"the X\" patterns\n- \"the mummer\" should be classified as an epithet, not a proper name\n- Epithets go through cross-group resolution (where death detection could work)\n\n## Output Files\n- HTML: output/masque_of_red_death/report.html\n- JSON: output/masque_of_red_death/analysis.json\n\n## Next Action\nRun PROMPT_fix.md to:\n1. First ADD DEBUG LOGGING to trace where the merge happens\n2. Then apply targeted fix based on findings\n","numLines":163,"startLine":1,"totalLines":163}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0196FyRWMFukFmsGd8N9JGd3","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Audiobook Prep Output Quality Rubric\n     2→\n     3→This rubric defines evaluation criteria for audiobook narrator preparation documents. Use this to assess analysis output quality.\n     4→\n     5→## Scoring Overview\n     6→\n     7→| Category | Weight | Focus |\n     8→|----------|--------|-------|\n     9→| Structure Detection | 20% | Chapter boundaries, front/back matter |\n    10→| Character Extraction | 25% | All characters identified, correct aliases |\n    11→| Character Profiles | 15% | Accurate descriptions, relationships |\n    12→| Chapter Summaries | 20% | Key events, narrator-useful details |\n    13→| Pronunciation Guide | 10% | Unusual words flagged, no false positives |\n    14→| HTML Presentation | 10% | Navigation, readability, organization |\n    15→\n    16→**Threshold:** Overall score >= 8.0 to pass\n    17→\n    18→---\n    19→\n    20→## Category Details\n    21→\n    22→### 1. Structure Detection (Weight: 20%)\n    23→\n    24→**What we're measuring:** Did the tool correctly identify the book's organization?\n    25→\n    26→| Score | Criteria |\n    27→|-------|----------|\n    28→| 10 | Perfect: All chapters identified, correct boundaries, handles unusual structures |\n    29→| 8-9 | Excellent: All major divisions correct, minor issues with epigraphs or breaks |\n    30→| 6-7 | Good: Most chapters correct, some boundary errors or missed subdivisions |\n    31→| 4-5 | Fair: Significant errors but majority of structure captured |\n    32→| 1-3 | Poor: Major structural errors, merged or missing chapters |\n    33→| 0 | Failed: Structure completely wrong or not detected |\n    34→\n    35→**Checklist:**\n    36→- [ ] All chapters detected (compare to expected chapter count)\n    37→- [ ] Chapter titles extracted correctly\n    38→- [ ] Front matter (dedication, preface) identified separately\n    39→- [ ] Back matter (notes, appendices) identified separately\n    40→- [ ] No merged chapters (two chapters treated as one)\n    41→- [ ] No split chapters (one chapter treated as multiple)\n    42→- [ ] Roman numerals handled correctly\n    43→- [ ] \"Chapter\" vs \"Book\" vs \"Part\" hierarchy correct\n    44→\n    45→---\n    46→\n    47→### 2. Character Extraction (Weight: 25%)\n    48→\n    49→**What we're measuring:** Are all significant characters identified with correct information?\n    50→\n    51→| Score | Criteria |\n    52→|-------|----------|\n    53→| 10 | Perfect: All named characters, correct aliases, accurate relationships |\n    54→| 8-9 | Excellent: All major characters, most minor, few alias issues |\n    55→| 6-7 | Good: Major characters correct, some missing minors or alias errors |\n    56→| 4-5 | Fair: Main characters present but significant gaps or errors |\n    57→| 1-3 | Poor: Major characters missing or incorrectly merged/split |\n    58→| 0 | Failed: Character extraction completely wrong |\n    59→\n    60→**Checklist:**\n    61→- [ ] All major characters (>10 mentions) identified\n    62→- [ ] No false character splits (same person as two entries)\n    63→- [ ] No false character merges (two people as one entry)\n    64→- [ ] Aliases correctly grouped (full name = nickname = title+name)\n    65→- [ ] Titles handled correctly (Dr., Mr., Mrs., etc.)\n    66→- [ ] Nicknames linked to canonical names\n    67→- [ ] No hallucinated characters (entries that don't exist in text)\n    68→- [ ] First appearance chapter is accurate\n    69→\n    70→**Common Issues:**\n    71→- Characters sharing surnames incorrectly merged (spouses, siblings)\n    72→- Full names and nicknames not linked\n    73→- Titled references (Mr. X) not linked to full names\n    74→- Minor characters filtered out too aggressively\n    75→\n    76→---\n    77→\n    78→### 3. Character Profiles (Weight: 15%)\n    79→\n    80→**What we're measuring:** Are character descriptions accurate and useful for narration?\n    81→\n    82→| Score | Criteria |\n    83→|-------|----------|\n    84→| 10 | Perfect: Rich, accurate descriptions with voice notes and relationships |\n    85→| 8-9 | Excellent: Good descriptions, accurate relationships, useful details |\n    86→| 6-7 | Good: Basic descriptions correct, some missing depth |\n    87→| 4-5 | Fair: Descriptions present but thin or partially inaccurate |\n    88→| 1-3 | Poor: Descriptions wrong or mostly missing |\n    89→| 0 | Failed: No useful profile information |\n    90→\n    91→**Checklist:**\n    92→- [ ] Physical descriptions match text (when provided)\n    93→- [ ] Personality traits supported by text evidence\n    94→- [ ] Key relationships identified (family, romantic, professional)\n    95→- [ ] No invented details (information not in the source text)\n    96→- [ ] Speech patterns or dialect noted (when present)\n    97→- [ ] Character arc summary useful for narrator\n    98→\n    99→---\n   100→\n   101→### 4. Chapter Summaries (Weight: 20%)\n   102→\n   103→**What we're measuring:** Do summaries help a narrator prepare for each chapter?\n   104→\n   105→| Score | Criteria |\n   106→|-------|----------|\n   107→| 10 | Perfect: Accurate, comprehensive, narrator-focused summaries |\n   108→| 8-9 | Excellent: Captures key events and tone, useful for prep |\n   109→| 6-7 | Good: Main events correct, some gaps or minor errors |\n   110→| 4-5 | Fair: Partially accurate but missing key events or has errors |\n   111→| 1-3 | Poor: Major inaccuracies or missing most content |\n   112→| 0 | Failed: Summaries wrong or not generated |\n   113→\n   114→**Checklist:**\n   115→- [ ] Key plot events captured (3-5 per chapter minimum)\n   116→- [ ] Characters appearing in chapter mentioned\n   117→- [ ] No hallucinated events (things that didn't happen)\n   118→- [ ] No major spoilers from future chapters\n   119→- [ ] Tone and mood indicated (useful for narrator)\n   120→- [ ] Length appropriate (100-300 words)\n   121→- [ ] Setting/location changes noted\n   122→\n   123→---\n   124→\n   125→### 5. Pronunciation Guide (Weight: 10%)\n   126→\n   127→**What we're measuring:** Are unusual words flagged with helpful pronunciation info?\n   128→\n   129→| Score | Criteria |\n   130→|-------|----------|\n   131→| 10 | Perfect: All unusual words flagged, accurate IPA, no false positives |\n   132→| 8-9 | Excellent: Good coverage, mostly accurate, few false positives |\n   133→| 6-7 | Good: Most unusual words caught, some gaps or errors |\n   134→| 4-5 | Fair: Partial coverage, noticeable gaps or many false positives |\n   135→| 1-3 | Poor: Major gaps or mostly incorrect |\n   136→| 0 | Failed: No useful pronunciation information |\n   137→\n   138→**Checklist:**\n   139→- [ ] Foreign words/phrases identified\n   140→- [ ] Unusual proper nouns flagged (names, places)\n   141→- [ ] Period-specific terms noted\n   142→- [ ] IPA provided and accurate (when present)\n   143→- [ ] No common words flagged (false positives)\n   144→- [ ] Context provided for homographs (read vs. read, lead vs. lead)\n   145→- [ ] Regional/dialect pronunciations noted\n   146→\n   147→---\n   148→\n   149→### 6. HTML Presentation (Weight: 10%)\n   150→\n   151→**What we're measuring:** Is the output professional and usable?\n   152→\n   153→| Score | Criteria |\n   154→|-------|----------|\n   155→| 10 | Perfect: Clean, navigable, print-ready, professional appearance |\n   156→| 8-9 | Excellent: Well-organized, easy to use, minor polish issues |\n   157→| 6-7 | Good: Functional and readable, some UX improvements possible |\n   158→| 4-5 | Fair: Usable but awkward navigation or formatting issues |\n   159→| 1-3 | Poor: Difficult to use, significant formatting problems |\n   160→| 0 | Failed: Broken or unreadable |\n   161→\n   162→**Checklist:**\n   163→- [ ] Navigation works (can jump to chapters, characters)\n   164→- [ ] Typography is readable\n   165→- [ ] Information is logically organized\n   166→- [ ] No broken elements or missing sections\n   167→- [ ] Prints cleanly (if applicable)\n   168→\n   169→---\n   170→\n   171→## Scoring Formula\n   172→\n   173→```\n   174→Overall = (\n   175→    Structure × 0.20 +\n   176→    Characters × 0.25 +\n   177→    Profiles × 0.15 +\n   178→    Summaries × 0.20 +\n   179→    Pronunciation × 0.10 +\n   180→    Presentation × 0.10\n   181→)\n   182→```\n   183→\n   184→**Thresholds:**\n   185→- **>= 8.0**: PASS - advance to next text\n   186→- **< 8.0**: FAIL - iterate with fixes\n   187→\n   188→---\n   189→\n   190→## Issue Severity Classification\n   191→\n   192→When scoring below threshold, classify issues for the fix phase:\n   193→\n   194→### CRITICAL (blocks progress, score impact > 1 point)\n   195→- Character merge/split errors for main characters\n   196→- Missing major characters\n   197→- Completely wrong chapter structure\n   198→- Hallucinated content\n   199→\n   200→### HIGH (significant impact, score impact 0.5-1 point)\n   201→- Missing minor but named characters\n   202→- Incorrect relationships for main characters\n   203→- Multiple chapter summary errors\n   204→- Major pronunciation gaps\n   205→\n   206→### MEDIUM (noticeable but manageable, score impact < 0.5)\n   207→- Minor character description inaccuracies\n   208→- Excessive false positives in pronunciation\n   209→- Formatting/presentation issues\n   210→- Missing some aliases\n   211→\n   212→### LOW (polish items)\n   213→- Minor wording improvements\n   214→- Edge case handling\n   215→- Performance optimizations\n   216→\n   217→---\n   218→\n   219→## Evaluation Notes\n   220→\n   221→### Use Your Knowledge\n   222→The evaluator (Claude) has literary knowledge of classic texts. Use this knowledge to:\n   223→- Verify character completeness against known casts\n   224→- Check summary accuracy against known plots\n   225→- Validate relationships and aliases\n   226→\n   227→### Be Specific\n   228→Instead of: \"Character extraction has issues\"\n   229→Write: \"Jay Gatsby and 'Gatsby' are listed as separate characters. These should be merged as aliases.\"\n   230→\n   231→### Be Actionable\n   232→Include file locations and suggested fixes when possible:\n   233→- \"Likely cause: alias resolution in src/agents/characters.py\"\n   234→- \"Suggested fix: improve fuzzy matching for FirstName-LastName to LastName\"\n   235→\n   236→### Focus on Threshold\n   237→If score is 7.8, focus only on what's needed to cross 8.0. Don't enumerate every possible improvement.\n   238→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3924aa2c-d6fe-4686-86bb-94b756a35690","uuid":"ee5d044a-0695-40b0-bc19-1d7b75598372","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/spec/output_quality.md","content":"# Audiobook Prep Output Quality Rubric\n\nThis rubric defines evaluation criteria for audiobook narrator preparation documents. Use this to assess analysis output quality.\n\n## Scoring Overview\n\n| Category | Weight | Focus |\n|----------|--------|-------|\n| Structure Detection | 20% | Chapter boundaries, front/back matter |\n| Character Extraction | 25% | All characters identified, correct aliases |\n| Character Profiles | 15% | Accurate descriptions, relationships |\n| Chapter Summaries | 20% | Key events, narrator-useful details |\n| Pronunciation Guide | 10% | Unusual words flagged, no false positives |\n| HTML Presentation | 10% | Navigation, readability, organization |\n\n**Threshold:** Overall score >= 8.0 to pass\n\n---\n\n## Category Details\n\n### 1. Structure Detection (Weight: 20%)\n\n**What we're measuring:** Did the tool correctly identify the book's organization?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 10 | Perfect: All chapters identified, correct boundaries, handles unusual structures |\n| 8-9 | Excellent: All major divisions correct, minor issues with epigraphs or breaks |\n| 6-7 | Good: Most chapters correct, some boundary errors or missed subdivisions |\n| 4-5 | Fair: Significant errors but majority of structure captured |\n| 1-3 | Poor: Major structural errors, merged or missing chapters |\n| 0 | Failed: Structure completely wrong or not detected |\n\n**Checklist:**\n- [ ] All chapters detected (compare to expected chapter count)\n- [ ] Chapter titles extracted correctly\n- [ ] Front matter (dedication, preface) identified separately\n- [ ] Back matter (notes, appendices) identified separately\n- [ ] No merged chapters (two chapters treated as one)\n- [ ] No split chapters (one chapter treated as multiple)\n- [ ] Roman numerals handled correctly\n- [ ] \"Chapter\" vs \"Book\" vs \"Part\" hierarchy correct\n\n---\n\n### 2. Character Extraction (Weight: 25%)\n\n**What we're measuring:** Are all significant characters identified with correct information?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 10 | Perfect: All named characters, correct aliases, accurate relationships |\n| 8-9 | Excellent: All major characters, most minor, few alias issues |\n| 6-7 | Good: Major characters correct, some missing minors or alias errors |\n| 4-5 | Fair: Main characters present but significant gaps or errors |\n| 1-3 | Poor: Major characters missing or incorrectly merged/split |\n| 0 | Failed: Character extraction completely wrong |\n\n**Checklist:**\n- [ ] All major characters (>10 mentions) identified\n- [ ] No false character splits (same person as two entries)\n- [ ] No false character merges (two people as one entry)\n- [ ] Aliases correctly grouped (full name = nickname = title+name)\n- [ ] Titles handled correctly (Dr., Mr., Mrs., etc.)\n- [ ] Nicknames linked to canonical names\n- [ ] No hallucinated characters (entries that don't exist in text)\n- [ ] First appearance chapter is accurate\n\n**Common Issues:**\n- Characters sharing surnames incorrectly merged (spouses, siblings)\n- Full names and nicknames not linked\n- Titled references (Mr. X) not linked to full names\n- Minor characters filtered out too aggressively\n\n---\n\n### 3. Character Profiles (Weight: 15%)\n\n**What we're measuring:** Are character descriptions accurate and useful for narration?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 10 | Perfect: Rich, accurate descriptions with voice notes and relationships |\n| 8-9 | Excellent: Good descriptions, accurate relationships, useful details |\n| 6-7 | Good: Basic descriptions correct, some missing depth |\n| 4-5 | Fair: Descriptions present but thin or partially inaccurate |\n| 1-3 | Poor: Descriptions wrong or mostly missing |\n| 0 | Failed: No useful profile information |\n\n**Checklist:**\n- [ ] Physical descriptions match text (when provided)\n- [ ] Personality traits supported by text evidence\n- [ ] Key relationships identified (family, romantic, professional)\n- [ ] No invented details (information not in the source text)\n- [ ] Speech patterns or dialect noted (when present)\n- [ ] Character arc summary useful for narrator\n\n---\n\n### 4. Chapter Summaries (Weight: 20%)\n\n**What we're measuring:** Do summaries help a narrator prepare for each chapter?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 10 | Perfect: Accurate, comprehensive, narrator-focused summaries |\n| 8-9 | Excellent: Captures key events and tone, useful for prep |\n| 6-7 | Good: Main events correct, some gaps or minor errors |\n| 4-5 | Fair: Partially accurate but missing key events or has errors |\n| 1-3 | Poor: Major inaccuracies or missing most content |\n| 0 | Failed: Summaries wrong or not generated |\n\n**Checklist:**\n- [ ] Key plot events captured (3-5 per chapter minimum)\n- [ ] Characters appearing in chapter mentioned\n- [ ] No hallucinated events (things that didn't happen)\n- [ ] No major spoilers from future chapters\n- [ ] Tone and mood indicated (useful for narrator)\n- [ ] Length appropriate (100-300 words)\n- [ ] Setting/location changes noted\n\n---\n\n### 5. Pronunciation Guide (Weight: 10%)\n\n**What we're measuring:** Are unusual words flagged with helpful pronunciation info?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 10 | Perfect: All unusual words flagged, accurate IPA, no false positives |\n| 8-9 | Excellent: Good coverage, mostly accurate, few false positives |\n| 6-7 | Good: Most unusual words caught, some gaps or errors |\n| 4-5 | Fair: Partial coverage, noticeable gaps or many false positives |\n| 1-3 | Poor: Major gaps or mostly incorrect |\n| 0 | Failed: No useful pronunciation information |\n\n**Checklist:**\n- [ ] Foreign words/phrases identified\n- [ ] Unusual proper nouns flagged (names, places)\n- [ ] Period-specific terms noted\n- [ ] IPA provided and accurate (when present)\n- [ ] No common words flagged (false positives)\n- [ ] Context provided for homographs (read vs. read, lead vs. lead)\n- [ ] Regional/dialect pronunciations noted\n\n---\n\n### 6. HTML Presentation (Weight: 10%)\n\n**What we're measuring:** Is the output professional and usable?\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 10 | Perfect: Clean, navigable, print-ready, professional appearance |\n| 8-9 | Excellent: Well-organized, easy to use, minor polish issues |\n| 6-7 | Good: Functional and readable, some UX improvements possible |\n| 4-5 | Fair: Usable but awkward navigation or formatting issues |\n| 1-3 | Poor: Difficult to use, significant formatting problems |\n| 0 | Failed: Broken or unreadable |\n\n**Checklist:**\n- [ ] Navigation works (can jump to chapters, characters)\n- [ ] Typography is readable\n- [ ] Information is logically organized\n- [ ] No broken elements or missing sections\n- [ ] Prints cleanly (if applicable)\n\n---\n\n## Scoring Formula\n\n```\nOverall = (\n    Structure × 0.20 +\n    Characters × 0.25 +\n    Profiles × 0.15 +\n    Summaries × 0.20 +\n    Pronunciation × 0.10 +\n    Presentation × 0.10\n)\n```\n\n**Thresholds:**\n- **>= 8.0**: PASS - advance to next text\n- **< 8.0**: FAIL - iterate with fixes\n\n---\n\n## Issue Severity Classification\n\nWhen scoring below threshold, classify issues for the fix phase:\n\n### CRITICAL (blocks progress, score impact > 1 point)\n- Character merge/split errors for main characters\n- Missing major characters\n- Completely wrong chapter structure\n- Hallucinated content\n\n### HIGH (significant impact, score impact 0.5-1 point)\n- Missing minor but named characters\n- Incorrect relationships for main characters\n- Multiple chapter summary errors\n- Major pronunciation gaps\n\n### MEDIUM (noticeable but manageable, score impact < 0.5)\n- Minor character description inaccuracies\n- Excessive false positives in pronunciation\n- Formatting/presentation issues\n- Missing some aliases\n\n### LOW (polish items)\n- Minor wording improvements\n- Edge case handling\n- Performance optimizations\n\n---\n\n## Evaluation Notes\n\n### Use Your Knowledge\nThe evaluator (Claude) has literary knowledge of classic texts. Use this knowledge to:\n- Verify character completeness against known casts\n- Check summary accuracy against known plots\n- Validate relationships and aliases\n\n### Be Specific\nInstead of: \"Character extraction has issues\"\nWrite: \"Jay Gatsby and 'Gatsby' are listed as separate characters. These should be merged as aliases.\"\n\n### Be Actionable\nInclude file locations and suggested fixes when possible:\n- \"Likely cause: alias resolution in src/agents/characters.py\"\n- \"Suggested fix: improve fuzzy matching for FirstName-LastName to LastName\"\n\n### Focus on Threshold\nIf score is 7.8, focus only on what's needed to cross 8.0. Don't enumerate every possible improvement.\n","numLines":238,"startLine":1,"totalLines":238}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0161djCgBFFZJNC8nxYFL3dv","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Oracle Loop Attempt 1 Summary\n     2→\n     3→**Purpose:** This document records what was tried during the first oracle loop run so future attempts can learn from these results and avoid repeating failed approaches.\n     4→\n     5→---\n     6→\n     7→## Executive Summary\n     8→\n     9→| Metric | Value |\n    10→|--------|-------|\n    11→| **Date Range** | January 17-18, 2026 |\n    12→| **Total Iterations** | 31 |\n    13→| **Texts Attempted** | Gatsby (5 attempts), Frankenstein (5 attempts) |\n    14→| **Texts Passed** | 0 |\n    15→| **Quality Threshold** | 8.0/10 |\n    16→\n    17→### Final Scores\n    18→- **Gatsby:** 5.15 → **4.05** (WORSE by 1.1 points) ❌\n    19→- **Frankenstein:** started at ~6.25 → **6.75** (minimal improvement) ❌\n    20→\n    21→### Key Finding\n    22→The loop made Gatsby **significantly worse** and Frankenstein barely better. The fundamental issue is that **fixes kept causing regressions** in other areas, and there was no mechanism to detect or rollback these regressions.\n    23→\n    24→---\n    25→\n    26→## Results by Text\n    27→\n    28→### Gatsby (5 attempts, FAILED)\n    29→\n    30→| Attempt | Score | Commit | Fix Description | Outcome |\n    31→|---------|-------|--------|-----------------|---------|\n    32→| 1 | 5.15 | - | Baseline run | Chapter count wrong (7 instead of 9) |\n    33→| 2 | 4.65 | `9bde9e5` | Prioritize hard boundary titles | ✓ Fixed chapters 7→9, ✗ Broke Wilson merge |\n    34→| 3 | 4.65 | `9a29656` | Prevent family member merging | ✗ Created James Gatz/Gatsby split |\n    35→| 4 | 4.65 | `31233a9` | Filter ambiguous last-name-only entries | ✗ ZERO impact |\n    36→| 5 | 4.05 | Unknown | Unknown changes | ✗ Chapter count regressed 9→6 |\n    37→\n    38→**Critical Issues Never Resolved:**\n    39→- James Gatz and Gatsby remained split (birth name vs known name)\n    40→- Narrator identified as \"elevator boy\" instead of Nick Carraway\n    41→- 626 pronunciation false positives\n    42→\n    43→### Frankenstein (5 attempts, FAILED)\n    44→\n    45→| Attempt | Score | Commit | Fix Description | Outcome |\n    46→|---------|-------|--------|-----------------|---------|\n    47→| 1 | N/A | `0d162f3` | CLI output path fix | ✓ SUCCESS |\n    48→| 2 | ~6.25 | `4e3cf44` | Relational descriptor candidate pairing | ✓ PARTIAL - creature/monster merged |\n    49→| 3 | 6.25 | `ba1215a` | Validation logic for relational merges | ✗ ZERO impact |\n    50→| 4 | 6.25 | `432f7c1` | Diagnostic logging only | ✗ WASTED - no actual fixes |\n    51→| 5 | 6.75 | `d501086` | Move relational pairing to execute first | ✗ ZERO impact |\n    52→\n    53→**Critical Issues Never Resolved:**\n    54→- Alphonse Frankenstein fragmented into 5 entries (\"my father\", \"Father\", \"M.\", \"M. Frankenstein\", \"Alphonse Frankenstein\")\n    55→- William Frankenstein missing from character list\n    56→- Henry Clerval split into 3 entries\n    57→- 664 pronunciation false positives (~50% common English words)\n    58→\n    59→---\n    60→\n    61→## Approaches That FAILED (DO NOT RETRY)\n    62→\n    63→### 1. Filter ambiguous last-name-only entries\n    64→**Commit:** `31233a9`\n    65→**Rationale:** Remove single-word names like \"Wilson\" when both \"George Wilson\" and \"Myrtle Wilson\" exist.\n    66→**Result:** ZERO impact on character merging.\n    67→**Why it failed:** The problem wasn't in candidate generation - the LLM was already rejecting merges.\n    68→\n    69→### 2. Block family member merges globally\n    70→**Commit:** `9a29656`\n    71→**Rationale:** Reject any merge where two names share a last name but have different first names.\n    72→**Result:** Over-aggressive - caused James Gatz to be split from Gatsby.\n    73→**Why it failed:** Too broad - blocked valid merges (birth names, aliases) along with invalid ones.\n    74→\n    75→```python\n    76→# This logic was TOO AGGRESSIVE:\n    77→if last1 == last2 and first1 != first2:\n    78→    return False  # Different first names, same last = different people\n    79→# Problem: \"James Gatz\" and \"Jay Gatsby\" have different first names but ARE the same person\n    80→```\n    81→\n    82→### 3. Adding diagnostic logging without implementing fixes\n    83→**Commit:** `432f7c1`\n    84→**Rationale:** Add logging to understand why merges weren't happening.\n    85→**Result:** WASTED ATTEMPT - logging added but never analyzed or acted upon.\n    86→**Lesson:** Don't commit diagnostic-only changes. Analyze, then fix, then commit.\n    87→\n    88→### 4. Moving relational pairing code position\n    89→**Commit:** `d501086`\n    90→**Rationale:** The relational pairing code wasn't executing because earlier stages filled the max_pairs limit.\n    91→**Result:** ZERO impact - pairs ARE now generated, but LLM still rejects the merges.\n    92→**Why it failed:** The bottleneck is NOT pair generation. The LLM merge decision is rejecting valid pairs.\n    93→\n    94→### 5. Validation logic changes for relational descriptors\n    95→**Commit:** `ba1215a`\n    96→**Rationale:** Allow merges with no shared words if they're relational descriptors with chapter overlap.\n    97→**Result:** ZERO impact.\n    98→**Why it failed:** Validation was already allowing the pairs. The LLM merge decision is the bottleneck.\n    99→\n   100→---\n   101→\n   102→## Approaches That WORKED (PRESERVE)\n   103→\n   104→### 1. Creature/monster/wretch epithet merging\n   105→**Commit:** `4e3cf44` (partial success)\n   106→**What it did:** Added relational/descriptive term detection to candidate pair generation.\n   107→**Result:** Successfully merged \"the creature\", \"the monster\", \"the wretch\" as aliases.\n   108→**Status:** PRESERVED through all subsequent attempts.\n   109→\n   110→```python\n   111→# This worked for descriptive epithets:\n   112→DESCRIPTIVE_PATTERNS = {\n   113→    'creature', 'monster', 'fiend', 'wretch', 'stranger', ...\n   114→}\n   115→# Successfully paired \"the creature\" with \"the monster\" for LLM evaluation\n   116→# LLM correctly decided they were the same character\n   117→```\n   118→\n   119→### 2. CLI output path fix\n   120→**Commit:** `0d162f3`\n   121→**What it did:** Ensured parent directories exist for CLI output paths.\n   122→**Result:** SUCCESS - CLI now respects explicit `--html` and `--output` flags.\n   123→\n   124→---\n   125→\n   126→## Root Cause Analysis\n   127→\n   128→### Why fixes kept failing\n   129→\n   130→The diagnostic logging from attempt 4 revealed:\n   131→\n   132→1. **Candidate pairs ARE being generated** - The pairing code works\n   133→2. **Validation logic IS allowing them** - Pairs pass validation\n   134→3. **LLM pairwise merge decision is REJECTING them** - This is the actual bottleneck\n   135→\n   136→The LLM (qwen3-next:80b) is asked \"Are 'my father' and 'Alphonse Frankenstein' the same person?\" and says NO, despite them clearly being the same character.\n   137→\n   138→**Possible causes:**\n   139→- Insufficient context provided to LLM for merge decision\n   140→- LLM prompt doesn't emphasize narrative context\n   141→- Model may need few-shot examples of relational descriptor merges\n   142→\n   143→### Why fixes caused regressions\n   144→\n   145→1. **Coupled components:** Character extraction and chapter detection share code in `consensus.py`\n   146→2. **No integration testing:** Unit tests pass but full pipeline breaks\n   147→3. **No regression gate:** Score drops weren't detected or acted upon\n   148→\n   149→---\n   150→\n   151→## Unresolved Core Issues\n   152→\n   153→### Character Fragmentation\n   154→| Issue | Example | Root Cause |\n   155→|-------|---------|------------|\n   156→| Relational descriptors | \"my father\" ≠ \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" | LLM rejecting valid merges |\n   157→| Title variations | \"M. Clerval\" ≠ \"Clerval\" | LLM rejecting valid merges |\n   158→| Birth names | \"James Gatz\" ≠ \"Gatsby\" | LLM rejecting valid merges |\n   159→\n   160→### Missing Characters\n   161→- William Frankenstein appears in chapter summaries but not character list\n   162→- Likely filtered by mention count threshold\n   163→\n   164→### Pronunciation False Positives\n   165→- 50%+ of flagged words are common English (my, man, father, old, young, child)\n   166→- No word frequency filtering implemented\n   167→- Should use a common English word list to filter\n   168→\n   169→---\n   170→\n   171→## Git Commits Reference\n   172→\n   173→### Character Extraction Fixes (src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py)\n   174→\n   175→| SHA | Date | Description | Impact |\n   176→|-----|------|-------------|--------|\n   177→| `d501086` | Jan 18 06:18 | Prioritize relational descriptor pairing | ZERO |\n   178→| `432f7c1` | Jan 18 05:07 | Add diagnostic logging | WASTED |\n   179→| `ba1215a` | Jan 18 03:45 | Allow relational merges with chapter overlap | ZERO |\n   180→| `4e3cf44` | Jan 18 02:36 | Enhance alias candidate pair generation | PARTIAL ✓ |\n   181→| `cd860e0` | Jan 18 02:33 | Block family member merges (early validation) | Unknown |\n   182→| `9bf7131` | Jan 17 21:28 | Debug logging for character merging | None |\n   183→| `31233a9` | Jan 17 21:34 | Filter ambiguous last-name-only entries | ZERO |\n   184→| `9a29656` | Jan 17 20:21 | Prevent family member merging | REGRESSION |\n   185→\n   186→### Chapter Detection Fixes (src/pipeline/chapter_detection/)\n   187→\n   188→| SHA | Date | Description | Impact |\n   189→|-----|------|-------------|--------|\n   190→| `9bde9e5` | Jan 17 20:18 | Prioritize hard boundary titles | REGRESSION (9→6 chapters) |\n   191→\n   192→### CLI Fixes (src/cli.py)\n   193→\n   194→| SHA | Date | Description | Impact |\n   195→|-----|------|-------------|--------|\n   196→| `0d162f3` | Jan 17 18:43 | Ensure parent dirs for output paths | SUCCESS ✓ |\n   197→\n   198→---\n   199→\n   200→## Code Snippets\n   201→\n   202→### Family Member Blocking (FAILED - Too Aggressive)\n   203→**Commit:** `9a29656`\n   204→```python\n   205→# Added to _validate_merge() in consensus.py\n   206→# Problem: This blocked valid merges like \"James Gatz\" + \"Jay Gatsby\"\n   207→\n   208→def get_first_last(words: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str]:\n   209→    titles = {'mr', 'mrs', 'ms', 'miss', 'dr', 'sir', 'lady', 'lord'}\n   210→    start_idx = 1 if (words[0].rstrip('.').lower() in titles and len(words) > 1) else 0\n   211→    first = words[start_idx] if start_idx < len(words) else \"\"\n   212→    last = words[-1] if len(words) > start_idx else \"\"\n   213→    return first, last\n   214→\n   215→first1, last1 = get_first_last(words1)\n   216→first2, last2 = get_first_last(words2)\n   217→\n   218→# THIS WAS TOO AGGRESSIVE:\n   219→if first1 and last1 and first2 and last2:\n   220→    if last1 == last2 and first1 != first2:\n   221→        return False, 0.05  # Rejected merges that should have been allowed\n   222→```\n   223→\n   224→### Relational Descriptor Pairing (PARTIAL SUCCESS)\n   225→**Commit:** `4e3cf44`\n   226→```python\n   227→# Added to _candidate_pairs_for_merge() in consensus.py\n   228→# This WORKED for creature/monster, but failed for family relations\n   229→\n   230→RELATIONAL_TERMS = {\n   231→    'father', 'mother', 'son', 'daughter', 'brother', 'sister',\n   232→    'uncle', 'aunt', 'grandfather', 'grandmother', 'cousin',\n   233→    'husband', 'wife', 'friend', 'companion',\n   234→}\n   235→\n   236→DESCRIPTIVE_PATTERNS = {\n   237→    'creature', 'monster', 'fiend', 'wretch', 'stranger',  # These WORKED\n   238→    'captain', 'professor', 'doctor', 'detective',\n   239→}\n   240→\n   241→# Pairs generated correctly, but LLM rejected family relation merges\n   242→# while accepting creature/monster merges\n   243→```\n   244→\n   245→---\n   246→\n   247→## Recommendations for Next Attempt\n   248→\n   249→### Priority 1: Investigate LLM Merge Decisions\n   250→The pairs are generated and validated. The LLM is rejecting them. Need to:\n   251→- Examine the prompt given to LLM for merge decisions\n   252→- Add more context (chapter summaries, surrounding text)\n   253→- Consider few-shot examples of relational descriptor merges\n   254→\n   255→### Priority 2: Add Pronunciation Word Frequency Filter\n   256→This is a separate, isolated fix that won't affect character extraction:\n   257→- Use a common English word list (top 5000-10000 words)\n   258→- Filter out common words before pronunciation flagging\n   259→- Should immediately improve pronunciation score from 3/10 to 6-7/10\n   260→\n   261→### Priority 3: Implement Regression Protection\n   262→Before any fix:\n   263→1. Record baseline score\n   264→2. Apply fix\n   265→3. Run full evaluation\n   266→4. If score drops > 0.3 points, auto-revert\n   267→\n   268→### Priority 4: Preserve What Works\n   269→- Keep creature/monster/wretch epithet merging\n   270→- Keep CLI output path fix\n   271→- Don't modify chapter detection until character issues are resolved\n   272→\n   273→---\n   274→\n   275→## Lessons Learned\n   276→\n   277→1. **Unit tests are not enough** - All 444 tests passed while output quality degraded\n   278→2. **One fix can break multiple things** - Character and chapter detection are coupled\n   279→3. **Diagnostic logging without action wastes attempts** - Analyze and fix in the same iteration\n   280→4. **The LLM is the bottleneck** - For character merging, the LLM merge decision is what's failing\n   281→5. **Regression protection is essential** - The loop needs to detect and rollback harmful changes\n   282→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3924aa2c-d6fe-4686-86bb-94b756a35690","uuid":"194d7ed2-6876-430f-b0b0-b9002223e073","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/spec/oracle-loop/ATTEMPT_1_SUMMARY.md","content":"# Oracle Loop Attempt 1 Summary\n\n**Purpose:** This document records what was tried during the first oracle loop run so future attempts can learn from these results and avoid repeating failed approaches.\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Date Range** | January 17-18, 2026 |\n| **Total Iterations** | 31 |\n| **Texts Attempted** | Gatsby (5 attempts), Frankenstein (5 attempts) |\n| **Texts Passed** | 0 |\n| **Quality Threshold** | 8.0/10 |\n\n### Final Scores\n- **Gatsby:** 5.15 → **4.05** (WORSE by 1.1 points) ❌\n- **Frankenstein:** started at ~6.25 → **6.75** (minimal improvement) ❌\n\n### Key Finding\nThe loop made Gatsby **significantly worse** and Frankenstein barely better. The fundamental issue is that **fixes kept causing regressions** in other areas, and there was no mechanism to detect or rollback these regressions.\n\n---\n\n## Results by Text\n\n### Gatsby (5 attempts, FAILED)\n\n| Attempt | Score | Commit | Fix Description | Outcome |\n|---------|-------|--------|-----------------|---------|\n| 1 | 5.15 | - | Baseline run | Chapter count wrong (7 instead of 9) |\n| 2 | 4.65 | `9bde9e5` | Prioritize hard boundary titles | ✓ Fixed chapters 7→9, ✗ Broke Wilson merge |\n| 3 | 4.65 | `9a29656` | Prevent family member merging | ✗ Created James Gatz/Gatsby split |\n| 4 | 4.65 | `31233a9` | Filter ambiguous last-name-only entries | ✗ ZERO impact |\n| 5 | 4.05 | Unknown | Unknown changes | ✗ Chapter count regressed 9→6 |\n\n**Critical Issues Never Resolved:**\n- James Gatz and Gatsby remained split (birth name vs known name)\n- Narrator identified as \"elevator boy\" instead of Nick Carraway\n- 626 pronunciation false positives\n\n### Frankenstein (5 attempts, FAILED)\n\n| Attempt | Score | Commit | Fix Description | Outcome |\n|---------|-------|--------|-----------------|---------|\n| 1 | N/A | `0d162f3` | CLI output path fix | ✓ SUCCESS |\n| 2 | ~6.25 | `4e3cf44` | Relational descriptor candidate pairing | ✓ PARTIAL - creature/monster merged |\n| 3 | 6.25 | `ba1215a` | Validation logic for relational merges | ✗ ZERO impact |\n| 4 | 6.25 | `432f7c1` | Diagnostic logging only | ✗ WASTED - no actual fixes |\n| 5 | 6.75 | `d501086` | Move relational pairing to execute first | ✗ ZERO impact |\n\n**Critical Issues Never Resolved:**\n- Alphonse Frankenstein fragmented into 5 entries (\"my father\", \"Father\", \"M.\", \"M. Frankenstein\", \"Alphonse Frankenstein\")\n- William Frankenstein missing from character list\n- Henry Clerval split into 3 entries\n- 664 pronunciation false positives (~50% common English words)\n\n---\n\n## Approaches That FAILED (DO NOT RETRY)\n\n### 1. Filter ambiguous last-name-only entries\n**Commit:** `31233a9`\n**Rationale:** Remove single-word names like \"Wilson\" when both \"George Wilson\" and \"Myrtle Wilson\" exist.\n**Result:** ZERO impact on character merging.\n**Why it failed:** The problem wasn't in candidate generation - the LLM was already rejecting merges.\n\n### 2. Block family member merges globally\n**Commit:** `9a29656`\n**Rationale:** Reject any merge where two names share a last name but have different first names.\n**Result:** Over-aggressive - caused James Gatz to be split from Gatsby.\n**Why it failed:** Too broad - blocked valid merges (birth names, aliases) along with invalid ones.\n\n```python\n# This logic was TOO AGGRESSIVE:\nif last1 == last2 and first1 != first2:\n    return False  # Different first names, same last = different people\n# Problem: \"James Gatz\" and \"Jay Gatsby\" have different first names but ARE the same person\n```\n\n### 3. Adding diagnostic logging without implementing fixes\n**Commit:** `432f7c1`\n**Rationale:** Add logging to understand why merges weren't happening.\n**Result:** WASTED ATTEMPT - logging added but never analyzed or acted upon.\n**Lesson:** Don't commit diagnostic-only changes. Analyze, then fix, then commit.\n\n### 4. Moving relational pairing code position\n**Commit:** `d501086`\n**Rationale:** The relational pairing code wasn't executing because earlier stages filled the max_pairs limit.\n**Result:** ZERO impact - pairs ARE now generated, but LLM still rejects the merges.\n**Why it failed:** The bottleneck is NOT pair generation. The LLM merge decision is rejecting valid pairs.\n\n### 5. Validation logic changes for relational descriptors\n**Commit:** `ba1215a`\n**Rationale:** Allow merges with no shared words if they're relational descriptors with chapter overlap.\n**Result:** ZERO impact.\n**Why it failed:** Validation was already allowing the pairs. The LLM merge decision is the bottleneck.\n\n---\n\n## Approaches That WORKED (PRESERVE)\n\n### 1. Creature/monster/wretch epithet merging\n**Commit:** `4e3cf44` (partial success)\n**What it did:** Added relational/descriptive term detection to candidate pair generation.\n**Result:** Successfully merged \"the creature\", \"the monster\", \"the wretch\" as aliases.\n**Status:** PRESERVED through all subsequent attempts.\n\n```python\n# This worked for descriptive epithets:\nDESCRIPTIVE_PATTERNS = {\n    'creature', 'monster', 'fiend', 'wretch', 'stranger', ...\n}\n# Successfully paired \"the creature\" with \"the monster\" for LLM evaluation\n# LLM correctly decided they were the same character\n```\n\n### 2. CLI output path fix\n**Commit:** `0d162f3`\n**What it did:** Ensured parent directories exist for CLI output paths.\n**Result:** SUCCESS - CLI now respects explicit `--html` and `--output` flags.\n\n---\n\n## Root Cause Analysis\n\n### Why fixes kept failing\n\nThe diagnostic logging from attempt 4 revealed:\n\n1. **Candidate pairs ARE being generated** - The pairing code works\n2. **Validation logic IS allowing them** - Pairs pass validation\n3. **LLM pairwise merge decision is REJECTING them** - This is the actual bottleneck\n\nThe LLM (qwen3-next:80b) is asked \"Are 'my father' and 'Alphonse Frankenstein' the same person?\" and says NO, despite them clearly being the same character.\n\n**Possible causes:**\n- Insufficient context provided to LLM for merge decision\n- LLM prompt doesn't emphasize narrative context\n- Model may need few-shot examples of relational descriptor merges\n\n### Why fixes caused regressions\n\n1. **Coupled components:** Character extraction and chapter detection share code in `consensus.py`\n2. **No integration testing:** Unit tests pass but full pipeline breaks\n3. **No regression gate:** Score drops weren't detected or acted upon\n\n---\n\n## Unresolved Core Issues\n\n### Character Fragmentation\n| Issue | Example | Root Cause |\n|-------|---------|------------|\n| Relational descriptors | \"my father\" ≠ \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" | LLM rejecting valid merges |\n| Title variations | \"M. Clerval\" ≠ \"Clerval\" | LLM rejecting valid merges |\n| Birth names | \"James Gatz\" ≠ \"Gatsby\" | LLM rejecting valid merges |\n\n### Missing Characters\n- William Frankenstein appears in chapter summaries but not character list\n- Likely filtered by mention count threshold\n\n### Pronunciation False Positives\n- 50%+ of flagged words are common English (my, man, father, old, young, child)\n- No word frequency filtering implemented\n- Should use a common English word list to filter\n\n---\n\n## Git Commits Reference\n\n### Character Extraction Fixes (src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py)\n\n| SHA | Date | Description | Impact |\n|-----|------|-------------|--------|\n| `d501086` | Jan 18 06:18 | Prioritize relational descriptor pairing | ZERO |\n| `432f7c1` | Jan 18 05:07 | Add diagnostic logging | WASTED |\n| `ba1215a` | Jan 18 03:45 | Allow relational merges with chapter overlap | ZERO |\n| `4e3cf44` | Jan 18 02:36 | Enhance alias candidate pair generation | PARTIAL ✓ |\n| `cd860e0` | Jan 18 02:33 | Block family member merges (early validation) | Unknown |\n| `9bf7131` | Jan 17 21:28 | Debug logging for character merging | None |\n| `31233a9` | Jan 17 21:34 | Filter ambiguous last-name-only entries | ZERO |\n| `9a29656` | Jan 17 20:21 | Prevent family member merging | REGRESSION |\n\n### Chapter Detection Fixes (src/pipeline/chapter_detection/)\n\n| SHA | Date | Description | Impact |\n|-----|------|-------------|--------|\n| `9bde9e5` | Jan 17 20:18 | Prioritize hard boundary titles | REGRESSION (9→6 chapters) |\n\n### CLI Fixes (src/cli.py)\n\n| SHA | Date | Description | Impact |\n|-----|------|-------------|--------|\n| `0d162f3` | Jan 17 18:43 | Ensure parent dirs for output paths | SUCCESS ✓ |\n\n---\n\n## Code Snippets\n\n### Family Member Blocking (FAILED - Too Aggressive)\n**Commit:** `9a29656`\n```python\n# Added to _validate_merge() in consensus.py\n# Problem: This blocked valid merges like \"James Gatz\" + \"Jay Gatsby\"\n\ndef get_first_last(words: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    titles = {'mr', 'mrs', 'ms', 'miss', 'dr', 'sir', 'lady', 'lord'}\n    start_idx = 1 if (words[0].rstrip('.').lower() in titles and len(words) > 1) else 0\n    first = words[start_idx] if start_idx < len(words) else \"\"\n    last = words[-1] if len(words) > start_idx else \"\"\n    return first, last\n\nfirst1, last1 = get_first_last(words1)\nfirst2, last2 = get_first_last(words2)\n\n# THIS WAS TOO AGGRESSIVE:\nif first1 and last1 and first2 and last2:\n    if last1 == last2 and first1 != first2:\n        return False, 0.05  # Rejected merges that should have been allowed\n```\n\n### Relational Descriptor Pairing (PARTIAL SUCCESS)\n**Commit:** `4e3cf44`\n```python\n# Added to _candidate_pairs_for_merge() in consensus.py\n# This WORKED for creature/monster, but failed for family relations\n\nRELATIONAL_TERMS = {\n    'father', 'mother', 'son', 'daughter', 'brother', 'sister',\n    'uncle', 'aunt', 'grandfather', 'grandmother', 'cousin',\n    'husband', 'wife', 'friend', 'companion',\n}\n\nDESCRIPTIVE_PATTERNS = {\n    'creature', 'monster', 'fiend', 'wretch', 'stranger',  # These WORKED\n    'captain', 'professor', 'doctor', 'detective',\n}\n\n# Pairs generated correctly, but LLM rejected family relation merges\n# while accepting creature/monster merges\n```\n\n---\n\n## Recommendations for Next Attempt\n\n### Priority 1: Investigate LLM Merge Decisions\nThe pairs are generated and validated. The LLM is rejecting them. Need to:\n- Examine the prompt given to LLM for merge decisions\n- Add more context (chapter summaries, surrounding text)\n- Consider few-shot examples of relational descriptor merges\n\n### Priority 2: Add Pronunciation Word Frequency Filter\nThis is a separate, isolated fix that won't affect character extraction:\n- Use a common English word list (top 5000-10000 words)\n- Filter out common words before pronunciation flagging\n- Should immediately improve pronunciation score from 3/10 to 6-7/10\n\n### Priority 3: Implement Regression Protection\nBefore any fix:\n1. Record baseline score\n2. Apply fix\n3. Run full evaluation\n4. If score drops > 0.3 points, auto-revert\n\n### Priority 4: Preserve What Works\n- Keep creature/monster/wretch epithet merging\n- Keep CLI output path fix\n- Don't modify chapter detection until character issues are resolved\n\n---\n\n## Lessons Learned\n\n1. **Unit tests are not enough** - All 444 tests passed while output quality degraded\n2. **One fix can break multiple things** - Character and chapter detection are coupled\n3. **Diagnostic logging without action wastes attempts** - Analyze and fix in the same iteration\n4. **The LLM is the bottleneck** - For character merging, the LLM merge decision is what's failing\n5. **Regression protection is essential** - The loop needs to detect and rollback harmful changes\n","numLines":282,"startLine":1,"totalLines":282}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0116EpfwYnnk3XsbchyzyCWm","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Audiobook Agent - Operational Guide\n     2→\n     3→This guide provides operational commands and codebase navigation for the autonomous improvement loop.\n     4→\n     5→## Running the Analysis Pipeline\n     6→\n     7→### Basic Commands\n     8→\n     9→```bash\n    10→# Full analysis on a text file with HTML output (use --rich-progress for live updates)\n    11→venv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt --html output/gatsby/report.html --output output/gatsby/analysis.json --rich-progress\n    12→\n    13→# With specific models per agent\n    14→venv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt \\\n    15→    --structure-model qwen2.5:14b \\\n    16→    --character-model qwen2.5:32b \\\n    17→    --summary-model qwen2.5:32b \\\n    18→    --pronunciation-model qwen2.5:14b \\\n    19→    --html output/gatsby/report.html \\\n    20→    --rich-progress\n    21→\n    22→# Set default model for all agents\n    23→venv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt --llm-model qwen2.5:32b --html output/gatsby/report.html --rich-progress\n    24→\n    25→# Adjust minimum character mentions threshold\n    26→venv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt --min-mentions 3 --html output/gatsby/report.html --rich-progress\n    27→```\n    28→\n    29→### CLI Options\n    30→\n    31→| Option | Description | Default |\n    32→|--------|-------------|---------|\n    33→| `--output FILE` | JSON output path | `{input_stem}.analysis.json` |\n    34→| `--html FILE` | HTML report path | None |\n    35→| `--tui` | Open interactive TUI after analysis | False |\n    36→| `--wpm N` | Words per minute for duration estimates | 150 |\n    37→| `--min-mentions N` | Minimum character mentions to include | 2 |\n    38→| `--llm-model MODEL` | Default model for all agents | System default |\n    39→| `--structure-model MODEL` | Model for chapter detection | Default |\n    40→| `--character-model MODEL` | Model for character extraction | Default |\n    41→| `--summary-model MODEL` | Model for chapter summaries | Default |\n    42→| `--pronunciation-model MODEL` | Model for pronunciation flags | Default |\n    43→| `--rich-progress` | Show live-updating progress display | False |\n    44→\n    45→## Output Locations\n    46→\n    47→```\n    48→output/\n    49→└── {book_name}/\n    50→    ├── report.html          # HTML report for narrator prep\n    51→    └── analysis.json        # Full JSON analysis data\n    52→```\n    53→\n    54→## Test Texts\n    55→\n    56→Available in `Test_Texts/`:\n    57→- `gatsby.txt` - The Great Gatsby (306 KB)\n    58→- `Frankenstein_ebook.txt` - Frankenstein (421 KB)\n    59→- `East of Eden - East Of Eden.pdf` - East of Eden (1.6 MB)\n    60→- `I_Have_No_Mouth_And_I_Must_Scream.pdf` - Ellison short story (325 KB)\n    61→- `One Hundred Years of Solitude - One_Hundred_Years_of_Solitude.pdf` - Marquez (1.5 MB)\n    62→- `See the Light V-15 11-11-25.docx` - Test document (1.2 MB)\n    63→\n    64→## Key Source Files\n    65→\n    66→### Agent System\n    67→| File | Purpose |\n    68→|------|---------|\n    69→| `src/agents/base.py` | Abstract Agent class with verify/refine methods |\n    70→| `src/agents/structure.py` | StructureAgent - chapter detection |\n    71→| `src/agents/characters.py` | CharacterAgent - character extraction & aliases |\n    72→| `src/agents/summaries.py` | SummaryAgent - chapter summaries |\n    73→| `src/agents/pronunciation.py` | PronunciationAgent - pronunciation flags |\n    74→| `src/agents/config.py` | OrchestratorConfig, per-agent model settings |\n    75→| `src/agents/validation.py` | Upstream validation types |\n    76→\n    77→### Pipeline\n    78→| File | Purpose |\n    79→|------|---------|\n    80→| `src/analyzer.py` | Main orchestrator - runs all agents |\n    81→| `src/pipeline/character_extraction/` | Character NER and extraction |\n    82→| `src/pipeline/character_profiling/` | Character profile generation |\n    83→| `src/pipeline/llm.py` | Unified LLM client (Ollama/OpenAI/Anthropic) |\n    84→| `src/pipeline/metrics.py` | Profiling and metrics |\n    85→\n    86→### Entry Points\n    87→| File | Purpose |\n    88→|------|---------|\n    89→| `src/cli.py` | CLI entry point (`audiobook-prep`) |\n    90→| `src/gui/desktop.py` | Tkinter GUI |\n    91→| `src/gui/tui.py` | Textual-based terminal UI |\n    92→\n    93→### Data Models\n    94→| File | Purpose |\n    95→|------|---------|\n    96→| `src/models.py` | Core Pydantic models (AnalysisResult, Character, etc.) |\n    97→| `src/pipeline/character_extraction/models.py` | Character extraction models |\n    98→| `src/pipeline/character_profiling/models.py` | Character profiling models |\n    99→\n   100→## Configuration\n   101→\n   102→### Agent Model Recommendations\n   103→\n   104→From `src/agents/config.py`:\n   105→\n   106→```python\n   107→RECOMMENDED_AGENT_MODELS = {\n   108→    \"structure\": [\"qwen2.5:14b\", \"qwen2.5:7b\", \"llama3.2\"],\n   109→    \"characters\": [\"qwen2.5:32b\", \"qwen2.5:72b\", \"llama3.1:70b\"],\n   110→    \"summaries\": [\"qwen2.5:32b\", \"qwen2.5:72b\", \"llama3.1:70b\"],\n   111→    \"pronunciation\": [\"qwen2.5:14b\", \"qwen2.5:32b\", \"llama3.2\"],\n   112→}\n   113→```\n   114→\n   115→### Verification Levels\n   116→\n   117→Agents support three verification levels:\n   118→- `STRUCTURAL` - Fast heuristic checks, no LLM\n   119→- `SELF_CHECK` - Local LLM validation, book-agnostic\n   120→- `ORACLE` - Claude-assisted (used by this improvement loop)\n   121→\n   122→## Common Issues & Fixes\n   123→\n   124→### Ollama Connection\n   125→```bash\n   126→# Ensure Ollama is running\n   127→ollama serve\n   128→\n   129→# Check available models\n   130→ollama list\n   131→\n   132→# Pull a model if needed\n   133→ollama pull qwen2.5:32b\n   134→```\n   135→\n   136→### Out of Memory\n   137→- Reduce model size: Use 14b instead of 32b\n   138→- Process shorter texts first\n   139→- Check GPU memory: `nvidia-smi`\n   140→\n   141→### Slow Analysis\n   142→- Full analysis of a novel can take 10-60 minutes\n   143→- Use smaller models for faster iteration during development\n   144→- Check `output/{book}/pipeline.log` for progress\n   145→\n   146→## Testing\n   147→\n   148→```bash\n   149→# Run all tests\n   150→pytest tests/ -v\n   151→\n   152→# Run specific agent tests\n   153→pytest tests/test_character_agent.py -v\n   154→pytest tests/test_structure_agent.py -v\n   155→pytest tests/test_summary_agent.py -v\n   156→pytest tests/test_pronunciation_agent.py -v\n   157→\n   158→# Run alias/merging tests\n   159→pytest tests/test_alias_merging.py -v\n   160→pytest tests/test_coreference.py -v\n   161→\n   162→# Run with coverage\n   163→pytest tests/ --cov=src --cov-report=html\n   164→```\n   165→\n   166→## Loop-Specific Files\n   167→\n   168→| File | Purpose |\n   169→|------|---------|\n   170→| `oracle-loop.sh` | Bash orchestrator for the improvement loop |\n   171→| `PROMPT_analyze.md` | Phase 1 instructions |\n   172→| `PROMPT_evaluate.md` | Phase 2 oracle evaluation instructions |\n   173→| `PROMPT_fix.md` | Phase 3 fix instructions |\n   174→| `manifest.json` | Test text tracking |\n   175→| `EVALUATION_STATE.md` | Current state and issues |\n   176→| `spec/output_quality.md` | Quality evaluation rubric |\n   177→| `logs/` | Iteration logs |\n   178→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3924aa2c-d6fe-4686-86bb-94b756a35690","uuid":"59743b53-3699-4901-bbbb-c0e309f38c3d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/AGENTS.md","content":"# Audiobook Agent - Operational Guide\n\nThis guide provides operational commands and codebase navigation for the autonomous improvement loop.\n\n## Running the Analysis Pipeline\n\n### Basic Commands\n\n```bash\n# Full analysis on a text file with HTML output (use --rich-progress for live updates)\nvenv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt --html output/gatsby/report.html --output output/gatsby/analysis.json --rich-progress\n\n# With specific models per agent\nvenv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt \\\n    --structure-model qwen2.5:14b \\\n    --character-model qwen2.5:32b \\\n    --summary-model qwen2.5:32b \\\n    --pronunciation-model qwen2.5:14b \\\n    --html output/gatsby/report.html \\\n    --rich-progress\n\n# Set default model for all agents\nvenv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt --llm-model qwen2.5:32b --html output/gatsby/report.html --rich-progress\n\n# Adjust minimum character mentions threshold\nvenv/bin/python -m src.cli analyze Test_Texts/gatsby.txt --min-mentions 3 --html output/gatsby/report.html --rich-progress\n```\n\n### CLI Options\n\n| Option | Description | Default |\n|--------|-------------|---------|\n| `--output FILE` | JSON output path | `{input_stem}.analysis.json` |\n| `--html FILE` | HTML report path | None |\n| `--tui` | Open interactive TUI after analysis | False |\n| `--wpm N` | Words per minute for duration estimates | 150 |\n| `--min-mentions N` | Minimum character mentions to include | 2 |\n| `--llm-model MODEL` | Default model for all agents | System default |\n| `--structure-model MODEL` | Model for chapter detection | Default |\n| `--character-model MODEL` | Model for character extraction | Default |\n| `--summary-model MODEL` | Model for chapter summaries | Default |\n| `--pronunciation-model MODEL` | Model for pronunciation flags | Default |\n| `--rich-progress` | Show live-updating progress display | False |\n\n## Output Locations\n\n```\noutput/\n└── {book_name}/\n    ├── report.html          # HTML report for narrator prep\n    └── analysis.json        # Full JSON analysis data\n```\n\n## Test Texts\n\nAvailable in `Test_Texts/`:\n- `gatsby.txt` - The Great Gatsby (306 KB)\n- `Frankenstein_ebook.txt` - Frankenstein (421 KB)\n- `East of Eden - East Of Eden.pdf` - East of Eden (1.6 MB)\n- `I_Have_No_Mouth_And_I_Must_Scream.pdf` - Ellison short story (325 KB)\n- `One Hundred Years of Solitude - One_Hundred_Years_of_Solitude.pdf` - Marquez (1.5 MB)\n- `See the Light V-15 11-11-25.docx` - Test document (1.2 MB)\n\n## Key Source Files\n\n### Agent System\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `src/agents/base.py` | Abstract Agent class with verify/refine methods |\n| `src/agents/structure.py` | StructureAgent - chapter detection |\n| `src/agents/characters.py` | CharacterAgent - character extraction & aliases |\n| `src/agents/summaries.py` | SummaryAgent - chapter summaries |\n| `src/agents/pronunciation.py` | PronunciationAgent - pronunciation flags |\n| `src/agents/config.py` | OrchestratorConfig, per-agent model settings |\n| `src/agents/validation.py` | Upstream validation types |\n\n### Pipeline\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `src/analyzer.py` | Main orchestrator - runs all agents |\n| `src/pipeline/character_extraction/` | Character NER and extraction |\n| `src/pipeline/character_profiling/` | Character profile generation |\n| `src/pipeline/llm.py` | Unified LLM client (Ollama/OpenAI/Anthropic) |\n| `src/pipeline/metrics.py` | Profiling and metrics |\n\n### Entry Points\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `src/cli.py` | CLI entry point (`audiobook-prep`) |\n| `src/gui/desktop.py` | Tkinter GUI |\n| `src/gui/tui.py` | Textual-based terminal UI |\n\n### Data Models\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `src/models.py` | Core Pydantic models (AnalysisResult, Character, etc.) |\n| `src/pipeline/character_extraction/models.py` | Character extraction models |\n| `src/pipeline/character_profiling/models.py` | Character profiling models |\n\n## Configuration\n\n### Agent Model Recommendations\n\nFrom `src/agents/config.py`:\n\n```python\nRECOMMENDED_AGENT_MODELS = {\n    \"structure\": [\"qwen2.5:14b\", \"qwen2.5:7b\", \"llama3.2\"],\n    \"characters\": [\"qwen2.5:32b\", \"qwen2.5:72b\", \"llama3.1:70b\"],\n    \"summaries\": [\"qwen2.5:32b\", \"qwen2.5:72b\", \"llama3.1:70b\"],\n    \"pronunciation\": [\"qwen2.5:14b\", \"qwen2.5:32b\", \"llama3.2\"],\n}\n```\n\n### Verification Levels\n\nAgents support three verification levels:\n- `STRUCTURAL` - Fast heuristic checks, no LLM\n- `SELF_CHECK` - Local LLM validation, book-agnostic\n- `ORACLE` - Claude-assisted (used by this improvement loop)\n\n## Common Issues & Fixes\n\n### Ollama Connection\n```bash\n# Ensure Ollama is running\nollama serve\n\n# Check available models\nollama list\n\n# Pull a model if needed\nollama pull qwen2.5:32b\n```\n\n### Out of Memory\n- Reduce model size: Use 14b instead of 32b\n- Process shorter texts first\n- Check GPU memory: `nvidia-smi`\n\n### Slow Analysis\n- Full analysis of a novel can take 10-60 minutes\n- Use smaller models for faster iteration during development\n- Check `output/{book}/pipeline.log` for progress\n\n## Testing\n\n```bash\n# Run all tests\npytest tests/ -v\n\n# Run specific agent tests\npytest tests/test_character_agent.py -v\npytest tests/test_structure_agent.py -v\npytest tests/test_summary_agent.py -v\npytest tests/test_pronunciation_agent.py -v\n\n# Run alias/merging tests\npytest tests/test_alias_merging.py -v\npytest tests/test_coreference.py -v\n\n# Run with coverage\npytest tests/ --cov=src --cov-report=html\n```\n\n## Loop-Specific Files\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `oracle-loop.sh` | Bash orchestrator for the improvement loop |\n| `PROMPT_analyze.md` | Phase 1 instructions |\n| `PROMPT_evaluate.md` | Phase 2 oracle evaluation instructions |\n| `PROMPT_fix.md` | Phase 3 fix instructions |\n| `manifest.json` | Test text tracking |\n| `EVALUATION_STATE.md` | Current state and issues |\n| `spec/output_quality.md` | Quality evaluation rubric |\n| `logs/` | Iteration logs |\n","numLines":178,"startLine":1,"totalLines":178}}}
