 uniquely colored rooms—each with stained-glass windows matching their decor—host a lavish masked ball, illuminated only by firelight from external tripods, with the final black chamber featuring blood-red windows and a massive ebony clock that chimes hourly, halting the festivities and instilling dread. As midnight strikes, a mysterious figure dressed as the Red Death, covered in blood and wearing a corpse-like mask, enters the ball, paralyzing the guests with terror; Prince Prospero, enraged, pursues the figure through all seven rooms with a dagger, only to collapse dead upon confronting it in the black chamber, revealing the figure to be an empty shell, as the plague claims every reveller and extinguishes all light and life.\\\"\\n    28→    }\\n    29→  ],\\n    30→  \\\"characters\\\": [\\n    31→    {\\n    32→      \\\"id\\\": \\\"char_the_prince_prospero_d85685e9\\\",\\n    33→      \\\"canonical_name\\\": \\\"the Prince Prospero\\\",\\n    34→      \\\"aliases\\\": [\\n    35→        \\\"Prospero\\\",\\n    36→        \\\"the mummer\\\"\\n    37→      ],\\n    38→      \\\"role\\\": null,\\n    39→      \\\"appearance\\\": null,\\n    40→      \\\"personality\\\": null,\\n    41→      \\\"voice_guidance\\\": null,\\n    42→      \\\"descriptions\\\": [\\n    43→        {\\n    44→          \\\"text\\\": \\\"Prince Prospero is a self-indulgent and authoritarian figure who isolates himself and a thousand courtiers in a fortified abbey to escape the Red Death, demonstrating a profound disregard for the suffering beyond his walls. His actions shift from arrogant detachment to frenzied rage when confronted by a figure embodying the plague he sought to evade, culminating in his violent pursuit and immediate death, revealing his hubris and inability to face mortality.\\\",\\n    45→          \\\"source_position\\\": 1894,\\n    46→          \\\"chapter_index\\\": null,\\n    47→          \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"llm_refined\\\"\\n    48→        }\\n    49→      ],\